{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-126850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/b37c10064003374c146c58bba99ff55304529ee66c9fb38c44246ccc90e8f402.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-126850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/c4c4f2f3d5b6188ac5a6ec41bb96fc0c7132cc39308cebfa16ee759eabac2c75.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-126850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f20b5fb16e8f912858002ed0dafa2fd7b9f7fbb3cc572064eb65dd6fd439bfe1"} {"question" "q-126850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-126850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-126850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-127053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/4d9663a0a40f3c3860cce0712e9e9aab41d55251ea15fca33ec663515af57d10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the rectangle $C,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-127053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{3}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=-i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/c6dd493e02633854184be1be0f38246e1e9dce2949f9cc1aa93b943c91d1e44c.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}({x}^{3}y\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (-i)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}-{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $-{x}^{3}y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-127053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f20b5fb16e8f912858002ed0dafa2fd7b9f7fbb3cc572064eb65dd6fd439bfe1"} {"question" "q-127053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-127053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x{y}^{2}"] ["math" "-{x}^{3}y"] ["math" "-{x}^{3}{y}^{2}"] ["math" "x{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-127053" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{x}^{3}y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/6f827dac7ecaf3a659245d1178aa39a519ae3a40339c917004247f12554c871a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/0b753318ebad9270c3fc8788d0e3949ffb29a800eb3f8dd6fb67cb271be68033.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=4\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-4,4].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-4}^{4}4\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 64=256.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226126" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f20b5fb16e8f912858002ed0dafa2fd7b9f7fbb3cc572064eb65dd6fd439bfe1"} {"question" "q-226126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "128"] ["math" "256"] ["math" "288"] ["math" "64"] ["math" "72"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226126" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "256"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/2dcfb8778adf7e9a05ff1dff886d0d8290427ed2ae910b46db4fe9aeb36170eb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+3y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/f283fe675e0a9ef736947e404f03ab5afb90a4209ba1a6949d18ac6d3fb20048.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=18.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=54.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 18+2\\cdot 54=144.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226133" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f20b5fb16e8f912858002ed0dafa2fd7b9f7fbb3cc572064eb65dd6fd439bfe1"} {"question" "q-226133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "144"] ["math" "156"] ["math" "164"] ["math" "176"] ["math" "180"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226133" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "144"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/80cc5e165f78bc1fc8cc7f83644fd00ca4a8f4e05e69d97b654bb2f1450d9b99.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i+3y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $6$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/assets/511bae61d710c3362bb18618717b3d44ef5dcedece5f669e0b5237b7b524a054.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-x\\,i+3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-12)+2\\cdot 36=48.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f20b5fb16e8f912858002ed0dafa2fd7b9f7fbb3cc572064eb65dd6fd439bfe1"} {"question" "q-226150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "32"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "42"] ["math" "48"] ["math" "54"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"} {"question" "q-226150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "48"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068094/state.json" "file_sha256" "f9ac1e64a27d353d3b504354b25708d6b954027dd4c47e7b30548583b1a08835"}] "usage" {"q-126850" []} "basis" 2279 "retractions" [[17592186179966 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/q-126850.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i + y^{2}j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O$, shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side $C_{1}$ is given by\n\n$∫_{C_{1}}AAAAAAAds$,\n\nwhat is the missing expression?"]] "immutable_